=== entry 651cc2734134
kind: policy
rules: delegate, background, store, pr-wait
intent: Workers spawn as async `delegate_task` children whose completions wake this thread; the program's tracker (`notes.md`) and files live in the shared pstack store under `orchestrate/<project-slug>/`; PRs are watched with `watch_pull_request` plus a heartbeat for merges. Policy (reload-the-orchestrator-after): reload the orchestrator after auto-compaction. The frontmatter PreToolUse hook records the coordinator's session id once; the project's settings.json SessionStart compact hook (a compact hook in skill frontmatter does not fire) prints a reload directive and the state file for that session only. This block names that state file, which the coordinator writes before it first ends the turn and rewrites after each wake, and references/compact-reload.md holds the settings snippet and script. Preserve no scripts, no polling loops, and no relaying between agents.
--- upstream
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `Task`. Keep the program's state in the Project tracker (`notes.md`) and the Project store. Watch each PR with PR and CI event subscriptions when your harness has them. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.
--- patched
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `delegate_task` (`mode: "async"`), each with its whole kickoff in `task` and its own `clientRequestId`, and keep each returned `taskId` and `childThreadId`. A worker's completion wakes this thread, so end the turn instead of polling. Keep the program's state in its tracker, `notes.md`, and its other files in the store, `~/.local/state/pstack/<repo>/orchestrate/<project-slug>/`, where `<repo>` is `basename "$(dirname "$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)")"`. Create it if missing. Watch each PR with `watch_pull_request`, then end the turn. It wakes this thread on a failed check, required checks passing, another person's comment or review, or a new conflict. A merge ends the watch without a wake, so pair it with a `schedule_task` heartbeat of 20 to 30 minutes while you wait for a merge, report its `nextRunAt`, and delete it with `delete_scheduled_task` once the merge lands. Call `unwatch_pull_request` before handing work back. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.

`$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/orchestrator-state.md` is the one file that survives compaction, so it lives in the project and not the store. Write it before you first end the turn, and rewrite it after each wake, under 6,000 characters. It holds the path to `notes.md`, the live agents with their `taskId`s, the Pods, the open PRs, the decisions waiting on the user, and the next steps. The compact hook in `references/compact-reload.md` prints it back; install that hook in the project's `.claude/settings.json`, or the file is never read.

=== entry ca155dd357b3
kind: translation
rules: delegate, resume, isolation, verify-surface, team-kit, transcripts, model-effort, cursor-only, same-model-review
intent: Writers get their own worktree and readers share the checkout, where upstream defaulted workers to a cloud VM; every worker runs on this machine, so the list of reasons to stay local becomes a note that runtime checks, transcripts, simulators, and local auth work from any worker. Effort is set under the role line's ceiling. No rule covers forking: T3 cannot fork a child's state, so new work that needs a worker's built state goes to a fresh `delegate_task` child working in that worker's worktree once the first stops writing. Messaging a worker is `t3_thread_send`. Policy: the verifier runs on its owner's model and effort unless the user names another (rule same-model-review). Preserve one owner per PR, the three-level cap, about ten in flight, never messaging to check on a worker, and starting with one unit.
--- upstream
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. Run workers on a new cloud VM (`environment: "cloud"`) unless the work needs this machine: a `control-ui` or `control-cli` runtime check (from `cursor-team-kit`), local transcripts, a simulator, or auth that exists only here. Pick each subagent's reasoning level and fast or standard speed for the job, per the Subagents section.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Fork when new work needs a worker's built state,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process. Message a worker only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on a different model family. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.
--- patched
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. Give a worker that writes `isolation: "worktree"`, a checkout of your current local commit, so commit first or name a pushed branch for its kickoff to check out. A worker that only reads gets no `isolation` and shares the checkout, with scratch copies in `/tmp`. Every worker runs on this machine, so a runtime check (the project's own verification skill, T3's `preview_*` tools, or the shell for a CLI), transcripts read with `t3_thread_read`, a simulator, and auth that exists only here work from any of them. Set each subagent's effort for the job per the Subagents section, lowering its line's effort for routine work and never raising it.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Hand new work that needs a worker's built state to a fresh worker there,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process: a `delegate_task` whose kickoff gives that worktree's absolute path and tells the new worker to work in it, once the first worker has stopped writing. Message a worker, with `t3_thread_send` to its `childThreadId`, only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on the same model and effort as the owner, unless the user named another. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.

=== entry b0d7f0bf067a
kind: translation
rules: stop
intent: A stalled worker is stopped with `task_cancel`, which stops what it delegated too, confirmed with `task_status`. Preserve judging a worker by its pushes and PR activity, the retry-by-cause list, the two-retry cap, and every other bullet verbatim.
--- upstream
- **Each kickoff names the evidence that proves the work is done,** such as a command and its expected output, a control-skill check, or an artifact. It also names the goal, the paths the worker may and may not write, and the actions it must not take. If you can't name the evidence, the unit isn't scoped yet.
- **Read the diff or the artifact, not only the worker's summary.** Green CI is an input to a verdict, not a verdict. Behavioral work needs more than a type check. A verdict covers one head SHA, so a new push needs a new verdict. Read a sample of each sub-coordinator's kickoffs while its workers run, and fix its instructions when one fails.
- **Send back work that drifts from scope or grows past what was asked.** Keep only the fixes that block the program, and park the rest as follow-ups.
- **Check a PR's live state before you report on it,** with `gh pr view` and `gh pr checks` or your forge's equivalents, not the worker's last report. Account for every worker you spawned as done, replaced, or absorbed into another worker's scope.
- **Stop or replace a worker that stalls or loops.** Judge it by its pushes and PR activity, not its status text. Retry by cause: a smaller scope after a context or memory limit, the same kickoff after a dropped connection, and a different model after a tool error. After two retries, drop the unit and replan around it. When a stopped worker returns late, check its result against the PR's current state, and hand any unique findings to a fresh worker.
- **Ask the user only for a real decision or a blocker,** such as an irreversible action, a product or preference call that no experiment settles, or a dead end that survived a replan. Rebase mechanics, retries, CI flakes, review triage, and "should I keep going" never reach the user.
--- patched
- **Each kickoff names the evidence that proves the work is done,** such as a command and its expected output, a control-skill check, or an artifact. It also names the goal, the paths the worker may and may not write, and the actions it must not take. If you can't name the evidence, the unit isn't scoped yet.
- **Read the diff or the artifact, not only the worker's summary.** Green CI is an input to a verdict, not a verdict. Behavioral work needs more than a type check. A verdict covers one head SHA, so a new push needs a new verdict. Read a sample of each sub-coordinator's kickoffs while its workers run, and fix its instructions when one fails.
- **Send back work that drifts from scope or grows past what was asked.** Keep only the fixes that block the program, and park the rest as follow-ups.
- **Check a PR's live state before you report on it,** with `gh pr view` and `gh pr checks` or your forge's equivalents, not the worker's last report. Account for every worker you spawned as done, replaced, or absorbed into another worker's scope.
- **Stop or replace a worker that stalls or loops.** Stop one with `task_cancel`, which also stops the tasks it delegated, and confirm with `task_status`. Judge it by its pushes and PR activity, not its status text. Retry by cause: a smaller scope after a context or memory limit, the same kickoff after a dropped connection, and a different model after a tool error. After two retries, drop the unit and replan around it. When a stopped worker returns late, check its result against the PR's current state, and hand any unique findings to a fresh worker.
- **Ask the user only for a real decision or a blocker,** such as an irreversible action, a product or preference call that no experiment settles, or a dead end that survived a replan. Rebase mechanics, retries, CI flakes, review triage, and "should I keep going" never reach the user.
